### Handling Multiple Channels with `select`
The `select` statement lets a goroutine wait on multiple communication operations simultaneously.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"time"
)

func main() {
	c1 := make(chan string)
	c2 := make(chan string)

	go func() {
		time.Sleep(1 * time.Second)
		c1 <- "result 1"
	}()
	go func() {
		time.Sleep(2 * time.Second)
		c2 <- "result 2"
	}()

	for i := 0; i < 2; i++ {
		select {
		case res1 := <-c1:
			fmt.Println("Received from c1:", res1)
		case res2 := <-c2:
			fmt.Println("Received from c2:", res2)
		}
	}
}
```

### The Scenario: Connection Pool Exhaustion
A background webhook worker spawns unconstrained goroutines for 100,000 incoming webhooks, immediately maxing out PostgreSQL connection pools (100 max) and triggering database errors.

### Production Worker Pool Solution
Cap maximum concurrent worker goroutines using buffered job channels and `context.Context` cancellation:

```go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"sync"
	"time"
)

type Job struct {
	ID int
}

func Worker(ctx context.Context, id int, jobs <-chan Job, wg *sync.WaitGroup) {
	defer wg.Done()
	for {
		select {
		case <-ctx.Done():
			return
		case job, ok := <-jobs:
			if !ok {
				return
			}
			fmt.Printf("Worker %d processing job %d\n", id, job.ID)
			time.Sleep(100 * time.Millisecond)
		}
	}
}
```

### Rate Limiting Web Requests in Go
Use `golang.org/x/time/rate` to implement a token bucket rate limiter for protecting backend APIs from surge traffic:

```go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"golang.org/x/time/rate"
	"time"
)

func main() {
	limiter := rate.NewLimiter(rate.Every(100*time.Millisecond), 5) // 10 req/sec, burst 5
	ctx := context.Background()

	for i := 1; i <= 10; i++ {
		err := limiter.Wait(ctx)
		if err != nil {
			break
		}
		fmt.Printf("Request %d processed at %v\n", i, time.Now().Format("15:04:05.000"))
	}
}
```
